Athleticism is best thought of as a stack of physical capacities, not one trait.

At the core, I’d break it into:

* Strength — how much force you can produce.
* Power — how quickly you can produce force; roughly strength × speed.
* Speed — how fast you can move, especially sprint velocity.
* Acceleration and deceleration — how fast you can start, stop, and control momentum.
* Agility / change of direction — redirecting force efficiently while staying balanced.
* Coordination — getting different body segments to work together at the right time.
* Balance and proprioception — knowing where your body is and controlling it in space.
* Mobility — usable range of motion under control, not just passive flexibility.
* Endurance / work capacity — sustaining output and recovering between efforts.
* Reaction and perception — seeing, interpreting, and responding to external information quickly.
* Elasticity / reactivity — using tendons and the stretch-shortening cycle efficiently: bounce, stiffness, spring.
* Durability / robustness — tolerating repeated force without breaking down.

Then there are two important overlays.

Movement skill is the software running on that hardware: sprint mechanics, jumping, landing, throwing, rotating, crawling, cutting, etc.

Sport-specific skill sits another layer above that: shooting a basketball, hitting a baseball, wrestling, catching, skating, and so on.

A useful compressed model is:

Athleticism = force + velocity + control + perception + energy + resilience.

And the highest-level athlete is not necessarily maxed out in every category. They have the right ratios for their sport and can rapidly shift between them—explode, brake, orient, react, recover, repeat.